keys ['back', 'basis', 'cite', 'front', 'grade', 'id', 'lesson']
LX1-c026 L03 | F: Assumption 1.1 (parity and deflation). What is the model's price index P_t, and what happens after period m? | B: P_t = (1 - delta)^min(t, m), with P_0 = 1, for 0 <= m <= H and 0 < delta < 1. The index falls by the fraction delta in each period on parity and is flat after period m. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c027 L03 | F: Definition 2.1: what is leverage L_t in the model? | B: The ratio L_t = V_t / N_t of the real debt V_t to the borrower's net worth N_t. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c028 L03 | F: In the model, what is the external-finance premium psi_t? | B: The rise in the extra cost of outside funds above its baseline: psi_t = phi (L_t - L_0), with slope phi > 0 (Assumption 1.3). | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c029 L03 | F: State the credit channel of a depression, as the model's chain (Definition 2.2). | B: A fall in the price index raises the real value of the fixed nominal debt (A1 and A2). The rise lowers borrower net worth one for one (A2). Higher leverage raises the premium (A3). The premium lowers output by f_t = beta psi_t, a fraction of baseline output, with output coefficient beta > 0 (A3). The chain describes the model and no economy outside it. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c030 L03 | F: Lemma 3.1. Hypotheses: C = N_0 + D; for D <= x < C, ell(x) = x / (C - x); Assumptions 1.2 and 1.3. What does L_t equal, and is ell increasing? | B: L_t = ell(V_t) whenever V_t < C, and L_0 = D / N_0. The function ell is strictly increasing on [D, C). | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 3 (Lemma 3.1)
LX1-c031 L03 | F: Proposition 3.2(a). Hypotheses: Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3, including the inequality V_m < N_0 + D of Assumption 1.2, with V_m = D (1 - delta)^(-m). What bound holds for the real debt over the horizon? | B: V_t <= V_m for every t from 0 to H. For t at most m, V_t = D (1 - delta)^(-t), which is strictly increasing when m >= 1. For t at least m, V_t = V_m. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.2(a))
LX1-c032 L03 | F: Proposition 3.2(b). Hypotheses: Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3. What is depth(m) in closed form? | B: depth(m) = beta phi (ell(V_m) - ell(D)) = beta phi (V_m / (N_0 + D - V_m) - D / N_0), with V_m = D (1 - delta)^(-m). | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.2(b))
LX1-c033 L03 | F: Proposition 3.2(c). Hypotheses: Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3. In which period is the maximum of the output fall first attained? | B: At t = m. When m >= 1, no earlier period attains the maximum. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.2(c))
LX1-c034 L03 | F: Under Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3, what is depth(0)? | B: Zero. With m = 0, P_t = 1 for every t, so V_t = D, L_t = L_0 and the premium is zero in every period. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proposition 3.2(c) and Problem P2
LX1-c035 L03 | F: Corollary 3.3. Hypotheses: Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3 for two parity durations m' and m with 0 <= m' < m <= H. What is the ordering of depth(m') and depth(m)? | B: depth(m') < depth(m): under these assumptions, the model economy with the longer parity duration has the strictly deeper fall. In Lab 1 (SIMULATED), economy X (m = 12) is therefore deeper than economy Y (m' = 4). | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 3 (Corollary 3.3)
LX1-c036 L03 | F: Corollary 3.4. Hypotheses: Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3 for two parity durations m' and m with 1 <= m' < m <= H. What is depth(m) / depth(m')? | B: (ell(V_m) - ell(D)) / (ell(V_m') - ell(D)). The right-hand side does not depend on beta or phi. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 3 (Corollary 3.4)
LX1-c037 L03 | F: Lab 1 Part B (SIMULATED, phi = beta = 1): what is economy X's output depth, and when is it first reached? | B: 9.20 percent of baseline output, first reached at t = 12. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED model values, printed to two decimals)
LX1-c038 L03 | F: Lab 1 Part B (SIMULATED, phi = beta = 1): what is economy Y's output depth, and when is it first reached? | B: 2.69 percent of baseline output, first reached at t = 4. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED model values, printed to two decimals)
LX1-c039 L03 | F: Lab 1 Part D (SIMULATED): what is the ratio of the depth of X to the depth of Y for beta phi = 1, to four decimals? | B: 3.4260. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1, Part D (SIMULATED); Corollary 3.4 (T)
LX1-c040 L03 | F: Definition 4.1: what is a lender of last resort? | B: A central bank that lends against collateral to institutions that are solvent but illiquid. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c041 L03 | F: Definition 4.1: what does it mean for an institution to be illiquid? | B: The institution cannot meet its payments from its own resources in the time available. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c042 L03 | F: Under Assumptions 1.1 to 1.3, does Proposition 3.2 say what a lender of last resort does to the premium? | B: No. Assumption 1.2 has no lending term, and Assumption 1.3 has no term through which a facility changes the premium of a solvent borrower. The proposition therefore says nothing about a facility. | cite [] | grade T | basis Remark 4.2 (reads Assumptions 1.2 and 1.3)
LX1-c043 L03 | F: Does the model show that bank failures caused the depth? | B: No. The model has one borrower and no bank (misconception 1, Section 6). Assumption 1.2 excludes default and any other change to the balance sheet, so no failure occurs. The proof of Proposition 3.2 uses none of these events. | cite [] | grade T | basis Assumption 1.2 and the proof of Proposition 3.2
LX1-c044 L03 | F: Does economy X's larger depth show that holding parity makes output fall further? | B: No. The ordering follows from the parity durations of 12 and 4 that Lab 1 assigns to economies X and Y under Assumption 1.1. The model cannot say whether holding parity causes a deeper fall. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof of Corollary 3.3; Lab 1 Part B
LX1-c045 L03 | F: Is it correct that a lender of last resort lends to any firm that asks, so that panics end? | B: No. Definition 4.1 requires collateral and limits lending to institutions that are solvent but illiquid. The model has no lending facility, so no panic result follows from it (Remark 4.2). | cite [] | grade T | basis Definition 4.1 and Remark 4.2 (misconception 3, Section 6)
LX1-c046 L03 | F: Is the 9.20 percent depth in Lab 1 a measured fall in output? | B: No. It is a SIMULATED model value under Assumption 1.3 with phi = beta = 1, and no output series enters the lab. Changing beta changes the depth in proportion, through the factor beta phi (Proposition 3.2(b)). | cite [] | grade T | basis Proposition 3.2(b); Lab 1 (SIMULATED)
LX1-c047 L03 | F: Does the L03 lab use an embedded historical series for gold-standard adherence or for output depth? | B: No. No redistributable historical series for either quantity is embedded, so the lab runs the stylised model and is labelled SIMULATED. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c048 L04 | F: Definition 1.1: what is the monthly log change at month t? | B: ell_t = ln(x_t / x_(t-1)), the natural logarithm of the ratio of two consecutive rates. It is a change per month and is not annualized. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c049 L04 | F: Definition 1.1: what is the dispersion of a window of monthly log changes, and when is it defined? | B: The sample standard deviation of the n changes, with denominator n - 1, about their mean. It is defined only when n >= 2. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c050 L04 | F: Proposition 1.2 (telescoping). Assuming (A1): the rates x_t are positive for every month from a to b, with a < b. What is the sum of ell_t for t from a + 1 to b? | B: ln(x_b / x_a). The sum has b - a terms. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 1 (Proposition 1.2)
LX1-c051 L04 | F: Is Lab 1's check of Proposition 1.2 an exact check in floating-point arithmetic? | B: No. Proposition 1.2 is an exact identity in real numbers. The lab checks it in floating point to a tolerance of 10^-12, which confirms the computation to that tolerance only. | cite [] | grade T | basis Remark 1.3; the tolerance SUM_TOLERANCE = 1e-12 in the Lab 1 code
LX1-c052 L04 | F: Proposition 2.2 (zero dispersion in the model). Hypotheses: (A2), x_t = kappa at every month of the window, with kappa > 0, and at least two monthly changes. What are the monthly log changes and the dispersion of the window? | B: Every monthly log change is 0, and the dispersion is 0. | cite [] | grade T | basis Proof in Section 2 (Proposition 2.2)
LX1-c053 L04 | F: In L04, what does Definition 2.1 contain? | B: An anchor currency, in which every rate is quoted; one foreign currency; a parity kappa > 0, the number of anchor units per unit of the foreign currency; and a market rate x_t > 0 at each month. A fixed-parity model is this model together with Assumption (A2), which holds the rate at the parity at every month. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c054 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part B (embedded GBP column, all 668 monthly changes from 1971-02 to 2026-09): which months have an exactly zero monthly change? | B: Three months: 1976-07, 2012-07 and 2019-11. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part B (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c055 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part C: what is the dispersion of window one, 1971-02 to 2026-09? | B: 0.027935 per month, from 668 monthly log changes (sample standard deviation with denominator 667). | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part C (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c056 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part C: what is the dispersion of window two, 2000-01 to 2026-09? | B: 0.024700 per month, from 321 monthly log changes. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part C (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c057 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part D: what is window two's dispersion as a multiple of window one's? | B: 0.8842 times window one, to four decimals. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part D (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c058 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part C: what is the largest absolute monthly log change in window two, in which month, and with what sign? | B: 0.096575 in absolute value, at 2008-10. The signed value is -0.096575. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part C (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c059 L04 | F: Does a currency with a parity have no monthly change in its recorded rate? | B: Not necessarily. Proposition 2.2 gives zero change in the model under (A2), the rate held at its parity every month. (A2) describes the model, not a recorded rate, which need not equal its parity at any month (Remark 2.3). | cite [] | grade T | basis Proposition 2.2 (model); Remark 2.3
LX1-c060 L04 | F: Does a smaller dispersion in window two show that the currency became calmer because of a regime change? | B: No. Lab 1 computes two sample standard deviations and their difference. It computes no test and names no cause, and the window start is a convention. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part D (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column); Problem E3 (the code contains no test)
LX1-c061 L04 | F: Is the monthly log change the same number as the simple percent change? | B: No. A rise from 1.00 to 1.10 has a simple change of 0.10 and a log change of ln 1.1 = 0.095310 to six decimals, a difference of 0.004690. | cite [] | grade T | basis Problem M1 (worked calculation)
LX1-c062 L04 | F: Does the fixed-parity model of Definition 2.1 state what the historical Bretton Woods arrangement was? | B: No. Definition 2.1 defines a model class, not the historical arrangement. L04 gives no definition of the historical Bretton Woods system, and it states no historical fact about its parities, participants or end. Section 4 lists what is left open. | cite [] | grade T | basis Definition 2.1 and Section 4
LX1-c063 L04 | F: Why does window two start at 2000-01 in Lab 1? | B: It is the first month from which the other two embedded snapshots, fx_daily and ust_daily, have data, since both begin on 2000-01-03. The start is a convention, and the lab does not test whether the difference survives another start. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c064 L04 | F: Which embedded dataset does Lab 1 read for its GBP column? | B: fx_monthly.csv: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, G.5/H.10 Foreign Exchange Rates (noon buying rates in New York), column GBP in US dollars per pound. Each value is the column's last value in its month. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c065 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part D: what is window two's dispersion minus window one's? | B: -0.003236. It is computed from the unrounded standard deviations, as Section 3 states. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part D (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c067 L03 | F: Lab 1 Part D (SIMULATED): does the ratio of the depths of X to Y change across the products beta phi = 1/2, 1 and 3? | B: No. The ratio is 3.4260 for each product, as Corollary 3.4 requires. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1, Part D (SIMULATED); Corollary 3.4 (T)
LX1-c068 L04 | F: Lab 1 Part B (embedded GBP column): how many monthly log changes in window one are nonzero? | B: 665 of the 668 changes are nonzero. | cite [] | grade C | basis Lab 1 Part B (fx_monthly, MANIFEST retrieved 2026-10-19; GBP column)
LX1-c069 L04 | F: What span of months does the embedded fx_monthly.csv cover? | B: 669 months, from 1971-01 to 2026-09, with one value per month: the column's last value in its month. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c070 L04 | F: Does the embedded fx_monthly.csv hold any interest-rate or forward-rate column? | B: No. It holds exchange-rate columns only, so the lab cannot test a relation between interest rates and exchange rates. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c071 L03 | F: In the model, how is borrower net worth N_t defined? | B: N_t = N_0 - (V_t - V_0), with N_0 the baseline net worth in cents. The whole rise in the real debt falls on net worth (Assumption 1.2). | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c072 L03 | F: In the model, how is the depth for parity duration m defined? | B: depth(m) = the maximum over 0 <= t <= H of f_t, where f_t = beta psi_t is the fall in output as a fraction of baseline output (Assumption 1.3). | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c075 L04 | F: When was the embedded fx_monthly.csv snapshot retrieved? | B: 2026-10-19, as its MANIFEST entry fx_monthly records. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c076 L03 | F: Definition 4.1: what does it mean for an institution to be solvent? | B: Its assets, valued as the lender values them, cover its liabilities. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
LX1-c077 L03 | F: Definition 4.1: what is a central bank? | B: The public institution that issues the monetary base. | cite [] | grade None | basis None
